#
# Per-Dockerfile ignore list for `-f examples/chatbot/Dockerfile`. BuildKit prefers
# "<dockerfile>.dockerignore" over the context root's .dockerignore, so this file REPLACES the root
# one for this image only and cannot affect the other five.
#
# It exists because the root .dockerignore excludes `examples/` and `*.md`, so this build fails
# today at three separate COPY lines -- `pyproject.toml README.md`, `requirements.txt` and
# `examples/chatbot/` -- with "not found", which reads like a typo in the Dockerfile rather than an
# ignore rule. Its sibling Dockerfile.mcp.dockerignore was added for the same reason.
#
# Deny-all-then-allow, NOT `!examples/chatbot/` bolted onto the root file, and the reason is the
# .env two lines below. Un-ignoring the directory in the ROOT file would re-include it for every
# image built from this context, and because `!` re-inclusion is last-match-wins it would override
# the root file's own `.env` / `.secrets/` lines -- reversing the exact protection those lines were
# added to provide.
*
!pyproject.toml
!README.md
!norviq
!examples/chatbot

# --- Re-exclusions. These MUST stay below the `!` lines: last matching pattern wins. ---

# THE IMPORTANT ONE. examples/chatbot/.env holds a real GROQ_API_KEY and a real NRVQ_API_TOKEN (it
# is gitignored, so it is present on developer machines and invisible to code review). `COPY
# examples/chatbot/ .` would bake it into a layer of an image that gets pushed to a registry, and
# app.py calls load_dotenv() at import, so it would also be READ at runtime: any variable the pod
# does not set -- NRVQ_CHATBOT_SYSTEM_PROMPT is one deployment.yaml omits -- would be silently
# supplied by the build machine's file instead of the pod's spec. The pod must get its identity
# from secretKeyRef and nowhere else.
examples/chatbot/.env
examples/chatbot/.env.*

# The developer's bytecode. CPython 3.12 on this host writes .pyc that CPython 3.12 in the image
# will load, so a stale cache can outlive an edit to the .py beside it. The root .dockerignore
# carries this rule; replacing that file means restating it.
**/__pycache__

# Deployment manifests and build files are inputs to the operator's workflow, not to the running
# container. Excluding them keeps `COPY examples/chatbot/ .` from shipping a k8s/ directory that
# only invites someone to think the image applies its own manifests.
examples/chatbot/k8s
examples/chatbot/Dockerfile
examples/chatbot/Dockerfile.*
